// The explanations of the rust edition's codes (0.27.0, front/rust,
// front/check/rust.vl, places/regions.vl): what a file read with Rust's
// meanings leaves to a later 0.27 release, the two seams of an edition
// that runs on MVS's std, and the borrows not checked yet.

use crate::tools::explain::Explain;

pub const EDITION_COUNT: i64 = 8;

pub static EDITION_TABLE: [Explain; 8] = [
    Explain {
        code: "edition.later",
        severity: "error",
        means: "The rust edition (`// EDITION: rust` on a program's first line, or `--edition \
            rust`) reads this Rust construct, but what it means comes with a later 0.27 release, \
            which the message names with the feature: `char` (0.27.2), `impl Trait` \
            in a return type, `where` bounds the checker does not take yet, tuple structs, \
            supertraits, `impl` for `&T`, associated constants, blanket impls and impls on an instance, `#[array_builtin(N)]` (0.27.2), a deferred `let x;` \
            and a write through a `*const T` (0.27.1). The error is soft: the rest of the \
            program is checked, and every such line is one count of the code.",
        fix: "Write it as 0.27.0 has it: a `u8` or a `u32` code point for a `char`, a generic \
            parameter for an `impl Trait` result, a struct with named fields for a tuple struct, `let x = value;`, a \
            `*mut T` to write through. `volec guide edition` lists what comes when.",
        example: "// EDITION: rust\n\
            fn adder(k: i64) -> impl Fn(i64) -> i64 { move |x| x + k }\n\
            \n\
            main.vl:2:21: error[edition.later] 'impl Fn(i64) -> i64' (impl_trait_return) comes with the rust edition in 0.27.2",
    },
    Explain {
        code: "edition.std_copy",
        severity: "error",
        means: "0.27.0-0.27.1 only (and VOLE_STD2=0): not raised on std2, Z4.1, where `*x` of \
            a value is rustc's E0614. In the rust edition, `*x` of a value that is not a reference. 0.27.0's edition \
            runs on MVS's std, whose getters (`get`, `last`, `first`, a map's `get`) give a copy \
            of the element where Rust's give `&T`: the `*` that Rust needs there has nothing to \
            read through. `for x in v.iter()` gives `&T`, as Rust's does.",
        fix: "Use the value as it is (the `try:` line): `*v.last().unwrap()` is \
            `v.last().unwrap()`. 0.27.2's std gives references.",
        example: "// EDITION: rust\n\
            s += *v.last().unwrap();\n\
            \n\
            main.vl:5:10: error[edition.std_copy] 'v.last().unwrap()' is a 'i64', not a reference: `*` takes a reference or a raw pointer\n\
            \x20 try: v.last().unwrap()",
    },
    Explain {
        code: "edition.std_by_value",
        severity: "error",
        means: "0.27.0-0.27.1 only (and VOLE_STD2=0): not raised on std2, Z4.1, whose functions \
            take references where Rust's do. In the rust edition, `&x` given where a value goes. 0.27.0's edition runs on \
            MVS's std, some of whose functions take an argument by value (`sink`: \
            `v.contains(x)` of a Vec of Strings, `m.insert(k, v)`) where Rust's take `&T`. A \
            `let` parameter of std's takes `&x` as Rust's does.",
        fix: "Pass the value: `x.clone()` to keep x (the `try:` line), `x` to move it, `*x` of a \
            copyable value. 0.27.2's std takes references.",
        example: "// EDITION: rust\n\
            if names.contains(&t) { .. }\n\
            \n\
            main.vl:8:23: error[edition.std_by_value] a reference where a 'String<Gpa>' goes: the parameter takes the value\n\
            \x20 try: t.clone()",
    },
    Explain {
        code: "edition.lenient",
        severity: "error",
        means: "(0.27.1) In the rust edition, a reference given where its value goes, or a \
            place where a reference goes, to a rust-edition function's parameter or a `let`'s \
            written type: 0.27.0 read the one as its value and lent the other, as MVS's 0.19 \
            meanings do; Rust does neither (rustc E0308). MVS std's parameters keep 0.19's \
            meanings until 0.27.2's std.",
        fix: "Rust's spelling (the `try:` line, `volec fix --suggested`): `*r` to read through \
            a reference, `&x` to lend a place, `x` for a `&x` where x's value goes.",
        example: "// EDITION: rust\n\
            fn takes(x: &i64) -> i64 { *x }\n\
            let c = takes(n);\n\
            \n\
            main.vl:3:15: error[edition.lenient] 'n' is a value where a reference goes\n\
            \x20 try: &n",
    },
    Explain {
        code: "borrowck.elision",
        severity: "error",
        means: "(0.27.1) A function's result holds a reference whose lifetime is not written, \
            and Rust's elision rules give it none: the function has more than one reference \
            region among its parameters (or none) and no `&self`/`&mut self` (the rules E1-E3, \
            docs/design/borrowck.md D4.2; rustc E0106).",
        fix: "Name the lifetime the result borrows from on that parameter and on the result: \
            `fn f<'a>(x: &'a T, y: &U) -> &'a T`; a result that borrows from nothing is \
            `&'static T` or an owned value.",
        example: "// EDITION: rust\n\
            fn longer(x: &i64, y: &i64) -> &i64 { x }\n\
            \n\
            main.vl:2:32: error[borrowck.elision] the result of 'longer' borrows, but from which parameter is not said: it has no single reference parameter and no `&self`",
    },
    Explain {
        code: "borrowck.unused_param",
        severity: "error",
        means: "(0.27.1) A struct's or enum's lifetime or type parameter that none of its \
            fields uses: its variance is unknown (bivariant, D5.3), so the borrow checker \
            cannot relate its regions (rustc E0392).",
        fix: "Use it in a field, or add a `PhantomData` field that says how the type uses it: \
            `PhantomData<&'a ()>` for a lifetime, `PhantomData<T>` for a type.",
        example: "// EDITION: rust\n\
            struct Tag<'a> { n: i64 }\n\
            \n\
            main.vl:2:12: error[borrowck.unused_param] the parameter 'a' of 'Tag' is never used in its fields",
    },
    Explain {
        code: "borrowck.static_mut_ref",
        severity: "error",
        means: "(0.27.1) A reference to a `static mut` (`&M`, `&mut M`): any code may write \
            the static at any time, so no reference to it can be checked (D6.2.5; Rust 2024's \
            static_mut_refs).",
        fix: "Take a raw pointer where you need it, `let p: *mut T = &mut S;`, and read or \
            write through it in `unsafe`.",
        example: "// EDITION: rust\n\
            static mut M: i64 = 1;\n\
            fn main() { let r = unsafe { &mut M }; }\n\
            \n\
            main.vl:3:30: error[borrowck.static_mut_ref] a reference to the `static mut` 'M': only its raw address may be taken",
    },
    Explain {
        code: "contract.move",
        severity: "error",
        means: "(0.27.1) A `requires` or an `ensures` moves a parameter or `result` in the rust \
            edition (C3.2, C3.3): a clause reads in place, and the function still has the value \
            after the check (the body, or the caller its result).",
        fix: "Read it or a part of it, or borrow it: `&x`, `x.len()`, `x.clone()`; or give the \
            type `#[derive(Clone, Copy)]` when it is plain data.",
        example: "// EDITION: rust\n\
            struct N { v: i64 }\n\
            fn eat(n: N) -> bool { n.v > 0 }\n\
            fn f(n: N) -> i64 requires eat(n) { n.v }\n\
            \n\
            main.vl:4:32: error[contract.move] a clause moves 'n': a `requires` or an `ensures` reads its parameters and `result` in place, and the function still has them",
    },
];
